```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.5)
library(FINN)
```

## What this vignette shows

Two things, end to end, on a single well-observed forest:

1. **FINN can be calibrated to PROFOUND data.** We take one PROFOUND site, turn its
   repeated tree census and local climate into FINN's input tables, and fit the
   model's demographic processes to the observed stand development.

2. **The fitted model can then be run under concrete management.** FINN's built-in
   `disturbance` argument only clearfells whole patches, so it cannot express
   thinning or target-diameter harvest. This vignette uses the prescriptive
   management layer (`thin_from_below()`, `target_diameter_harvest()`,
   `species_removal()`, `clearfell()`, driven by `simulate_managed()`) to impose
   real silvicultural regimes on the calibrated demography and compare their
   trajectories.

> **Read this before interpreting the scenarios.** A model fitted to a stand's
> observed history has not learned how the forest responds to a *new* silvicultural
> regime (altered light after thinning, planting, changed regeneration). A
> prescriptive run answers "given this fitted demography, what does removing these
> trees on this schedule do?" — a legitimate scenario, not a trained management
> response.

## The PROFOUND database

PROFOUND assembles long-term observations at European forest sites — tree censuses,
stand summaries, local and reanalysis climate — for benchmarking forest models. We
use the `ProfoundData` package to pull one site; here, the Norway spruce stand at
**Bílý Kříž** (Czech Republic).

```{r profound-pull, eval = FALSE}
library(ProfoundData)
setDB(path.expand("raw-data/ProfoundData.sqlite"))

trees   <- getData(dataset = "TREE",          site = "bily_kriz")
climate <- getData(dataset = "CLIMATE_LOCAL", site = "bily_kriz")
sites   <- getData(dataset = "SITES")
```

## From PROFOUND tables to FINN inputs

FINN needs an initial cohort table, an observed-state table, and an environment
(climate) array. The data-preparation helpers turn the PROFOUND tree list and
climate series into exactly those (see the *Preparing your data for FINN* vignette
for the general path).

```{r prep, eval = FALSE}
# tree list  -> cohorts + observed state ; climate -> env array
obs   <- makeObsData(trees, ...)          # observed dbh / trees per census
init  <- makeInitCohorts(trees, ...)      # starting cohorts at t0
envdf <- climateDF2array(climate, ...)    # temperature / precipitation drivers
```

## Fitting FINN

We assemble the processes and calibrate them jointly against the observed census.

```{r fit, eval = FALSE}
model <- finn(
  growth       = createProcess(~ temp + prec, FINN::growth),
  mortality    = createProcess(~ temp + prec, FINN::mortality),
  regeneration = createProcess(~ temp + prec, FINN::regeneration),
  ...
)
fit(model, obs = obs, env = envdf, init = init, epochs = ...)
```

```{r fit-check, eval = FALSE}
# calibration quality: predicted vs observed basal area on held-out years
```

## Simulating management scenarios

With a calibrated model we impose four regimes over a common horizon and compare
standing basal area and harvest removals. Each prescription is size- and/or
species-selective — none of them is expressible through FINN's clearfell-only
`disturbance`.

```{r scenarios, eval = FALSE}
schedule_thin <- data.frame(
  year = seq(10, 100, by = 10),
  prescription = I(list(thin_from_below(residual_ba = 30)))   # tend to 30 m2/ha
)

schedule_zsn <- data.frame(
  year = seq(5, 100, by = 5),
  prescription = I(list(target_diameter_harvest(dbh_target = 45, p = 1)))
)

schedule_convert <- data.frame(
  year = seq(10, 60, by = 10),
  prescription = I(list(species_removal(species = SPRUCE, p = 0.3)))
)

run_baseline <- simulateForest(model, env = envdf, patches = 20)                 # no intervention
run_thin     <- simulate_managed(model, envdf, 20, schedule_thin)
run_zsn      <- simulate_managed(model, envdf, 20, schedule_zsn)
run_convert  <- simulate_managed(model, envdf, 20, schedule_convert)
```

```{r scenarios-plot, eval = FALSE}
# standing basal area over time, one line per regime + cumulative harvest
```

## Takeaways

- PROFOUND's repeated census plus local climate is enough to calibrate FINN at a
  site.
- Concrete silviculture — thinning to a residual basal area, target-diameter
  harvest, gradual species conversion — runs on the fitted model through the
  prescriptive management layer, which FINN's clearfell `disturbance` cannot
  express on its own.
- The scenarios are conditional on demography learned from the observed (largely
  unmanaged) record; treat them as "what if we cut like this", not as a learned
  management response.

## References

Reyer, C. P. O., et al. (2020). The PROFOUND Database for evaluating vegetation
models and simulating climate impacts on European forests. *Earth System Science
Data*, 12, 1295–1320. <https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-12-1295-2020>

Pichler & Käber (2026). Inferring processes within dynamic forest models using
hybrid modelling. *Methods in Ecology and Evolution*.
<https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210x.70347>
